Subject: Typed-array element access for compiled code

What this does
  - The unit layout carries the class ids of the typed arrays a compiled body reads in
    place (Uint8Clamped, Int8, Uint8, Int16, Uint16, Int32, Uint32, Float32, Float64).
  - `qjs_aot_typed_put` stores a number into an in-range element of a typed array: an
    integer into any element type (as the interpreter's own fast path converts it), a
    double into the float types. It refuses an out-of-range index, an immutable buffer
    and anything else, and the caller takes the slow path.
  - Reads and the length need no engine code: a typed array keeps its element pointer
    and count where a dense array keeps its values and count.

Why we need it
  A loop over a typed array ran every element read, write and length through the
  engine's step handler, 1.5 to 2.9 times slower than the interpreter.

